WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.270
Machine

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:06.350
learning sounds so complicated,

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.310
but it's not so complicated.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.950
StatQuest.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.390
Hello, I'm Josh Starmer, and welcome to

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.550
StatQuest. Today, we're going to cover

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.310
AdaBoost, and it's going to be clearly

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.350
explained.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.670
Note, this StatQuest shows how to

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.710
combine AdaBoost with decision trees,

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.550
because that is the most common way to

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.630
use AdaBoost. So, if you're not familiar

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.470
with decision trees, check out the

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:33.870
quest.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.390
We will also mention random forests, so

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.390
if you don't know about them, check out

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:39.910
the quest.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.270
We'll start by using decision trees and

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.550
random forests to explain the three

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:47.310
concepts behind AdaBoost.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:48.750
Then, we'll get into the nitty-gritty

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.150
details of how AdaBoost creates a forest

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:53.470
of trees from scratch, and how it's used

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.230
to make classifications.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.670
So, let's start by using decision trees

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.030
and random forests to explain the three

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.950
main concepts behind AdaBoost.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.430
In a random forest, each time you make a

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.630
tree, you make a full-sized tree.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.110
Some trees might be bigger than others,

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.110
but there's no predetermined maximum

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:17.830
depth.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.710
In contrast, in a forest of trees made

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.910
with AdaBoost, the trees are usually

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.870
just a node and two leaves.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.470
Oh, no, it's the dreaded terminology

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.150
alert.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:33.870
A tree with just one node and two leaves

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:36.230
is called a stump.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.630
So, this is really a forest of stumps

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.910
rather than trees.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.270
Stumps are not great at making accurate

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.910
classifications.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.550
For example, if we were using this data

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.150
to determine if someone had heart

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.030
disease or not,

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:54.110
then a full-sized decision tree would

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.070
take advantage of all four variables

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.470
that we measured, chest pain, blood

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.470
circulation, blocked arteries, and

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.390
weight, to make a decision.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.750
But, a stump can only use one variable

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.670
to make a decision.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.710
Thus, stumps are technically weak

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:12.430
learners.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:14.910
However, that's the way AdaBoost likes

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:16.949
it, and it's one of the reasons why they

00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:19.949
are so commonly combined.

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:22.830
Now, back to the random forest.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.510
In a random forest, each tree has an

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.190
equal vote on the final classification.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.670
This tree's vote is worth just as much

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.070
as this tree's vote, or this tree's

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.830
vote.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.630
In contrast, in a forest of stumps made

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.390
with AdaBoost, some stumps get more say

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.630
in the final classification than others.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.630
In this illustration, the larger stumps

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:50.070
get more say in the final classification

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:53.230
than the smaller stumps.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.990
Lastly, in a random forest, each

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.310
decision tree is made independently of

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.190
the others.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:02.310
In other words, it doesn't matter if

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.550
this tree was made first, or this one.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.430
In contrast, in a forest of stumps made

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.310
with AdaBoost, order is important.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.229
The errors that the first stump makes

00:03:16.239 --> 00:03:19.830
influence how the second stump is made.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.630
And the errors that the second stump

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:22.870
makes

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.150
influence how the third stump is made.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.509
Etcetera, etcetera, etcetera.

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:33.150
To review, the three ideas behind

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.070
AdaBoost are:

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.870
One, AdaBoost combines a lot of weak

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:40.350
learners to make classifications.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:42.350
The weak learners are almost always

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:44.070
stumps.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.830
Two, some stumps get more say in the

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.790
classification than others.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.750
Three, each stump is made by taking the

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:56.510
previous stumps mistakes into account.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:59.310
Bam!

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:01.070
Now, let's dive into the nitty-gritty

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.110
detail of how to create a forest of

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.110
stumps using AdaBoost.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.230
First, we'll start with some data.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.030
We create a forest of stumps with

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.430
AdaBoost to predict if a patient has

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.590
heart disease.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:17.509
We will make these predictions based on

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:19.430
a patient's chest pain and blocked

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:22.950
artery status and their weight.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:24.710
The first thing we do is give each

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.470
sample a weight that indicates how

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:28.070
important it is to be correctly

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.470
classified.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.830
Note, the sample weight is different

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:34.710
from the patient weight, and I'll do the

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:36.710
best I can to be clear about which of

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.909
the two I'm talking about.

00:04:39.919 --> 00:04:42.470
At the start, all samples get the same

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:43.909
weight.

00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:45.990
One divided by the total number of

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:47.590
samples.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.030
In this case, that's one divided by

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.550
eight.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:53.710
And that makes the samples all equally

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:56.110
important.

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:58.950
However, after we make the first stump,

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:00.750
these weights will change in order to

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:03.670
guide how the next stump is created.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:05.909
In other words, we'll talk more about

00:05:05.919 --> 00:05:08.909
the sample weights later.

00:05:08.919 --> 00:05:10.670
Now, we need to make the first stump in

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.830
the forest.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.070
This is done by finding the variable

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:17.550
chest pain, blocked arteries, or patient

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:19.470
weight that does the best job

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:22.350
classifying the samples.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:24.550
Note, because all of the weights are the

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:28.830
same, we can ignore them right now.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:31.230
We start by seeing how well chest pain

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:34.070
classifies the samples.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.590
Of the five samples with chest pain,

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.470
three were correctly classified as

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:40.590
having heart disease.

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:44.310
And two were incorrectly classified.

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:47.350
Of the three samples without chest pain,

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:49.790
two were correctly classified as not

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.030
having heart disease.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:56.350
And one was incorrectly classified.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:58.350
Now we do the same thing for blocked

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.070
arteries.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:03.030
And for patient weight.

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:05.750
Note, we used the techniques described

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:07.950
in the decision tree StatQuest to

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:11.230
determine that 176 was the best weight

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:14.510
to separate the patients.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.950
Now we calculate the Gini index for the

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:19.550
three stumps.

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:22.230
The Gini index for patient weight is the

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:23.750
lowest.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:25.630
So this will be the first stump in the

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:27.909
forest.

00:06:27.919 --> 00:06:29.990
Now we need to determine how much say

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.510
this stump will have in the final

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:33.750
classification.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:36.150
Remember, some stumps get more say in

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:40.270
the final classification than others.

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.750
We determine how much say a stump has in

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:45.110
the final classification based on how

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:48.590
well it classified the samples.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:52.030
This stump made one error.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:55.990
This patient, who weighs less than 176,

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.550
has heart disease, but the stump says

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:01.190
they do not.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:03.670
The total error for a stump is the sum

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:05.590
of the weights associated with the

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.510
incorrectly classified samples.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.470
Thus, in this case, the total error is

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.790
1/8.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.390
Note, because all of the sample weights

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.070
add up to one, total error will always

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:23.430
be between zero for a perfect stump and

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:27.030
one for a horrible stump.

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:29.270
We use the total error to determine the

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.270
amount of say this stump has in the

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:33.790
final classification with the following

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:35.390
formula.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:39.390
Amount of say equals 1/2 times the log

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:42.030
of 1 minus the total error divided by

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:44.710
the total error.

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.110
We can draw a graph of the amount of say

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:48.750
by plugging in a bunch of numbers

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:52.430
between zero and one for total error.

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:54.990
The blue line tells us the amount of say

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:57.790
for total error values between zero and

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:59.590
one.

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:01.870
When a stump does a good job and the

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:04.350
total error is small,

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:06.510
then the amount of say is a relatively

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:10.070
large positive value.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:11.510
When a stump is no better at

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.950
classification than flipping a coin,

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:16.070
i.e. half the stumps are correctly

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:18.230
classified and half are incorrectly

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:20.909
classified, and the total error equals

00:08:20.919 --> 00:08:22.870
0.5,

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:26.710
then the amount of say will be zero.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:29.070
And when a stump does a terrible job and

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:31.950
the total error is close to one,

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:33.790
in other words, if the stump

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:35.750
consistently gives you the opposite

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:37.589
classification,

00:08:37.599 --> 00:08:39.990
then the amount of say will be a large

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:42.230
negative value.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:44.790
So if a stump votes for heart disease,

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:47.030
the negative amount of say will turn

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:51.110
that vote into not heart disease.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:54.670
Note, if total error is one or zero,

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:57.750
then this equation will freak out.

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:00.910
In practice, a small error term is added

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:03.710
to prevent this from happening.

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:07.070
With patient weight greater than 176,

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:10.110
the total error is 1/8, so we just plug

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:14.230
and chug.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:16.510
And the amount of say that this stump

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:21.270
has on the final classification is 0.97.

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:23.710
Bam!

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.470
Now that we've worked out how much say

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:27.430
this stump gets when classifying a

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:28.829
sample,

00:09:28.839 --> 00:09:30.829
let's work out how much say the chest

00:09:30.839 --> 00:09:33.030
pain stump would have if it had been the

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:34.950
best stump.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:37.510
Note, we don't need to do this, but I

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:39.350
think it helps illustrate the concepts

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.830
we've covered so far.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:45.350
Chest pain made three errors.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:47.630
And the total error equals the sum of

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:49.390
the weights for the incorrectly

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:54.070
classified samples.

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:56.630
So, the total error for chest pain is

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:58.910
3/8.

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:00.710
We can get a sense of what the amount of

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:03.190
say will be by looking at the graph when

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:06.310
total error equals 3/8.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:08.470
So, we are expecting the amount of say

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:12.670
to be between 0 and 0.5.

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:15.110
Now, we plug 3/8 into the formula for

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:21.630
the amount of say and do the math.

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:23.390
And the amount of say that the chest

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:25.430
pain stump would have had on the final

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:29.790
classification is 0.42.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:31.990
I'll leave the blocked artery stump as

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:34.910
an exercise for the viewer.

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:36.910
Now, we know how the sample weights for

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:39.510
the incorrectly classified samples are

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:41.990
used to determine the amount of say each

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:43.910
stump gets.

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:46.190
Bam!

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:48.030
Now, we need to learn how to modify the

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:50.310
weights so that the next stump will take

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:52.630
the errors that the current stump made

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:55.270
into account.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:57.150
Let's go back to the first stump that we

00:10:57.160 --> 00:10:59.310
made.

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:01.590
When we created this stump, all of the

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:04.430
sample weights were the same.

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:06.630
And that meant we did not emphasize the

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:09.030
importance of correctly classifying any

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:11.590
particular sample.

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.470
But, since this stump incorrectly

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:16.350
classified this sample,

00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:18.430
we will emphasize the need for the next

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:20.710
stump to correctly classify it by

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:23.910
increasing its sample weight

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:26.270
and decreasing all of the other sample

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:28.670
weights.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:31.030
Let's start by increasing the sample

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:33.390
weight for the incorrectly classified

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:35.710
sample.

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:37.670
This is the formula we will use to

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:39.710
increase the sample weight for the

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:43.430
sample that was incorrectly classified.

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:45.230
We plug in the sample weight from the

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:47.070
last stump

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:51.790
and we scale 1/8 with this term.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:53.510
To get a better understanding of how

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:55.750
this part will scale the previous sample

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:58.990
weight, let's draw a graph.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:01.750
The blue line is equal to e raised to

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:04.110
the amount of say.

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:06.110
When the amount of say is relatively

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:09.350
large, i.e. the last stump did a good

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:11.950
job classifying samples,

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:13.830
then we will scale the previous sample

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:16.870
weight with a large number.

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:19.070
This means that the new sample weight

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:22.950
will be much larger than the old one.

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:25.030
And when the amount of say is relatively

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:28.190
low, i.e. the last stump did not do a

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:31.390
very good job classifying samples,

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:33.270
then the previous sample weight is

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:36.829
scaled by a relatively small number.

00:12:36.839 --> 00:12:39.310
This means that the new sample weight

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:41.390
will only be a little larger than the

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:43.630
old one.

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:46.350
In this example, the amount of say was

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:48.950
0.97.

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:52.390
And e raised to the 0.97

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:55.710
equals 2.64.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:58.070
That means the new sample weight is

00:12:58.080 --> 00:12:59.870
0.33,

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:02.550
which is more than the old one.

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:04.430
Bam.

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:06.350
Now we need to decrease the sample

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:08.230
weights for all of the correctly

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:10.990
classified samples.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:12.950
This is the formula we will use to

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:16.190
decrease the sample weights.

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:18.510
The big difference is the negative sign

00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:22.070
in front of amount of say.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:24.590
Just like before, we plug in the sample

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:26.390
weight.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:28.430
And just like before, we can get a

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:30.190
better understanding of how this will

00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:32.350
scale the sample weight by plotting a

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:34.790
graph using different values for amount

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:36.950
of say.

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:39.950
The blue line represents e raised to the

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:42.870
negative amount of say.

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:44.870
When the amount of say is relatively

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:46.470
large,

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:48.510
then we scale the sample weight by a

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:51.070
value close to zero.

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:53.150
This will make the new sample weight

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:55.750
very small.

00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:57.830
If the amount of say for the last stump

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:00.150
is relatively small,

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:02.350
then we will scale the sample weight by

00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:05.030
a value close to one.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:07.390
This means that the new sample weight

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:09.150
will be just a little smaller than the

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:11.350
old one.

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:13.750
In this example, the amount of say was

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:16.310
0.97.

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:19.990
And e raised to the negative 0.97

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:23.430
equals 0.38.

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:26.710
The new sample weight is 0.05,

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:29.870
which is less than the old one.

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:31.829
Bam.

00:14:31.839 --> 00:14:33.670
We will keep track of the new sample

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:36.750
weights in this column.

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:39.030
We plug in 0.33

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:40.870
for the sample that was incorrectly

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:42.950
classified.

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:47.790
All of the other samples get 0.05.

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:49.870
Now we need to normalize the new sample

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:53.350
weights so that they will add up to one.

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:55.550
Right now, if you add up the new sample

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:59.630
weights, you get 0.68.

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:02.190
So we divide each new sample weight by

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:03.829
0.68

00:15:03.839 --> 00:15:07.110
to get the normalized values.

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:09.110
Now, when we add up the new sample

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:11.710
weights, we get one, plus or minus a

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:14.390
little rounding error.

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:16.230
Now we just transfer the normalized

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:18.110
sample weights to the sample weights

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:20.670
column, since those are what we will use

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:23.390
for the next stump.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:25.350
Now we can use the modified sample

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:27.750
weights to make the second stump in the

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:29.510
forest.

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:32.550
Bam!

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:34.670
In theory, we could use the sample

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:36.590
weights to calculate weighted Gini

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:39.110
indexes to determine which variable

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:41.910
should split the next stump.

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:43.950
The weighted Gini index would put more

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:46.110
emphasis on correctly classifying this

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:47.190
sample,

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:48.990
the one that was misclassified by the

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:51.510
last stump, since this sample has the

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:54.830
largest sample weight.

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:57.110
Alternatively, instead of using a

00:15:57.120 --> 00:15:59.790
weighted Gini index, we can make a new

00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:01.830
collection of samples that contains

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:03.990
duplicate copies of the samples with the

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:06.990
largest sample weights.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:09.830
So we start by making a new, but empty,

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:12.350
data set that is the same size as the

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:14.310
original.

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:16.670
Then we pick a random number between 0

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:18.750
and 1.

00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:21.030
And we see where that number falls when

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:22.790
we use the sample weights like a

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:25.190
distribution.

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:28.710
If the number is between 0 and 0.7,

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:30.710
then we would put this sample into the

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:33.750
new collection of samples.

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:36.590
And if the number is between 0.7 and

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:38.590
0.14,

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:40.470
then we would put this sample into the

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:42.950
new collection of samples.

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:45.870
And if the number is between 0.14

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:47.829
and 0.21,

00:16:47.839 --> 00:16:49.710
then we would put this sample into the

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:52.230
new collection of samples.

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:55.070
And if the number is between 0.21

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:57.150
and 0.70,

00:16:57.160 --> 00:16:59.070
then we would put this sample into the

00:16:59.080 --> 00:17:01.670
new collection of samples.

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:04.990
Et cetera, et cetera.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:07.390
For example, imagine the first number I

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:10.590
picked was 0.72.

00:17:10.600 --> 00:17:12.870
Then I would put this sample into my new

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:15.870
collection of samples.

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:17.910
Then I pick another random number and

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:20.910
get 0.42.

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:23.150
And I would put this sample into my new

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:25.790
collection of samples.

00:17:25.800 --> 00:17:28.870
Then I pick 0.83.

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:30.870
And I would put this sample into my new

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:33.470
collection of samples.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:36.470
Then I pick 0.51.

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:38.510
And I would put this sample into my new

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:41.510
collection of samples.

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:44.190
Note, this is the second time we have

00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:46.470
added this particular sample to the new

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:49.110
collection of samples.

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:51.630
We then continue to pick random numbers

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:53.950
and add samples to the new collection

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:55.790
until the new collection is the same

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:58.710
size as the original.

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:01.190
Ultimately, this sample was added to the

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:03.790
new collection of samples four times,

00:18:03.800 --> 00:18:07.910
reflecting its larger sample weight.

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:11.270
Now we get rid of the original samples

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:15.110
and use the new collection of samples.

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:17.870
Lastly, we give all of the samples equal

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:21.670
sample weights, just like before.

00:18:21.680 --> 00:18:23.790
However, that doesn't mean the next

00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:25.710
stump will not emphasize the need to

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:29.110
correctly classify these samples.

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:31.630
Because these samples are all the same,

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:33.430
they will be treated as a block,

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:35.470
creating a large penalty for being

00:18:35.480 --> 00:18:38.510
misclassified.

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:40.670
Now we go back to the beginning and try

00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:42.910
to find the stump that does the best job

00:18:42.920 --> 00:18:44.670
classifying the new collection of

00:18:44.680 --> 00:18:46.750
samples.

00:18:46.760 --> 00:18:48.670
So that is how the errors that the first

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:50.190
tree makes

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:53.870
influence how the second tree is made.

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:55.790
And how the errors that the second tree

00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:56.950
makes

00:18:56.960 --> 00:19:00.150
influence how the third tree is made.

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:03.630
Etcetera, etcetera, etcetera.

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:06.750
Double bam!

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:08.710
Now we need to talk about how a forest

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:11.470
of stumps created by AdaBoost makes

00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:13.630
classifications.

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:15.750
Imagine that these stumps classified a

00:19:15.760 --> 00:19:19.230
patient as has heart disease.

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:21.350
And these stumps classified the patient

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:25.470
as does not have heart disease.

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:27.710
These are the amounts of say for these

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:29.230
stumps.

00:19:29.240 --> 00:19:31.270
And these are the amounts of say for

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:33.750
these stumps.

00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:35.750
Now we add up the amounts of say for

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:37.830
this group of stumps

00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:40.950
and for this group of stumps.

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:43.910
Ultimately, the patient is classified as

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:46.350
has heart disease because this is the

00:19:46.360 --> 00:19:48.870
larger sum.

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:51.910
Triple bam!

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:54.510
To review, the three ideas behind

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:56.550
AdaBoost are

00:19:56.560 --> 00:19:59.350
one, AdaBoost combines a lot of weak

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:01.830
learners to make classifications.

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:04.150
The weak learners are almost always

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:05.710
stumps.

00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:08.310
Two, some stumps get more say in the

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:11.270
classification than others.

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:14.390
And three, each stump is made by taking

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:16.590
the previous stumps mistakes into

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:18.430
account.

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:20.590
If we have a weighted gini function,

00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:22.830
then we use it with the sample weights.

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:25.070
Otherwise, we use the sample weights to

00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:27.510
make a new data set that reflects those

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:29.670
weights.

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:31.830
Hooray! We've made it to the end of

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:34.390
another exciting StatQuest. If you like

00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:36.310
this StatQuest and want to see more,

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:37.910
please subscribe.

00:20:37.920 --> 00:20:39.790
And if you want to support StatQuest,

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:41.950
well, consider buying a t-shirt or a

00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:43.750
hoodie, or buying one or two of my

